Final answer: ### 1. Task outcome (short version):
Extracted structured PICO evidence from 5 included studies on adjuvant progestagen therapy for endometrial cancer. Four 
RCTs showed no significant survival benefit; one small prospective trial reported a significant survival advantage 
(P<0.001).

### 2. Task outcome (extremely detailed version):
Detailed extraction:
Study 1 (n=856): Multicentre RCT of MPA vs no treatment/radiotherapy in stage I endometrial cancer; no difference in 
relapse-free survival at 84 months.
Study 5 (n=1148): RCT of adjuvant progestagen vs no additional therapy in stage I/II; similar crude survival/relapse 
rates; higher intercurrent death in progestagen group (P=0.04); longer median survival for cancer-related deaths (30 vs 
22 months, P=0.03); possible benefit in high-risk subgroup.
Study 7 (n=429): RCT of progestogens added to primary surgery/radiotherapy; no significant difference in 5-year survival
(projected 76% overall).
Study 9 (n=35): Small placebo-controlled RCT of hydroxyprogesterone for 14 weeks post-op in stage IA/IB; no survival or 
recurrence benefit at 5 years.
Study 10 (n=205): Single-institution prospective RCT of adjuvant progestagen vs no additional therapy; significantly 
longer survival in progestagen group (P<0.001).
Overall, most trials do not support a clear survival benefit, though one small trial and a high-risk subgroup analysis 
suggest potential advantage that warrants further investigation. Safety signals include increased non-cancer deaths in 
one trial.

### 3. Additional context (if relevant):
Citations were not provided in the input data; 'Not provided' used as placeholder. Effect measures (HR/RR/OR) were not 
reported in any abstract, so numerical effect sizes are largely absent except for median survival difference in Study 5.
Where p-values were given, they have been included. Some outcomes (e.g., relapse rates) had no p-value in the abstract 
and are listed with null p_value. The extracted JSON follows the required schema and can be directly integrated into the
systematic review data.

